<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-elementary-operations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Elementary Operations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The strategy for solving a system is to replace it, step by step, with a simpler
      system that has exactly the same solutions. This section identifies the three
      operations that are guaranteed to preserve the solution set.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-equivalent-systems">
    <title>Equivalent Systems</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Two systems of linear equations are <term>equivalent</term> if they have
        <em>exactly the same</em> solutions.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="eg-equivalent-systems">
    <title>Two Equivalent Systems</title>
    <p>
      The following systems of linear equations have the same solutions and hence they
      are equivalent:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \begin{array}{rcl} 2x - y \amp = \amp 1 \\ x - y \amp = \amp 0 \end{array}
          \qquad \text{and} \qquad
          \begin{array}{rcl} x + y \amp = \amp 2 \\ y \amp = \amp 1 \end{array}
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Indeed, each has the single solution <m>x = y = 1</m>. The second system is
      visibly easier to read the solution off of, and that is the whole idea: we solve a
      system of linear equations by using <em>elementary operations</em> to transform the
      system into an equivalent but simpler system from which the solution can be easily
      obtained.
    </p>
  </example>

  <definition xml:id="def-elementary-operations">
    <title>Elementary Operations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following three operations on a system of linear equations are called
        <term>elementary operations</term>.
        <ol>
          <li><p><term>Interchange:</term> interchange two equations.</p></li>
          <li><p><term>Scaling:</term> multiply an equation by a nonzero number.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Replacement:</term> replace an equation by the sum of itself and a
              multiple of a <em>different</em> equation. (Briefly: add a multiple of one
              equation to another.)
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    A shorthand for the three operations saves a great deal of writing. With
    <m>E_i</m> standing for the <m>i</m>th equation of the system, we write
    <md>
      <mrow>
        E_i \leftrightarrow E_j \amp \qquad \text{interchange equations } i \text{ and } j,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        k \, E_i \amp \qquad \text{multiply equation } i \text{ by } k \neq 0,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        k \, E_j + E_i \amp \qquad \text{add } k \text{ times equation } j \text{ to equation } i.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    In a replacement <m>k\,E_j + E_i</m>, the equation written <em>last</em>,
    <m>E_i</m>, is the one that changes; equation <m>j</m> is only read, and stays as it
    was. A replacement is also called an <term>elimination</term> step, because its
    usual purpose is to remove a variable from equation <m>i</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The whole method of this chapter rests on one fact: these operations never change
    the solution set.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="rem-operations-reversible">
    <title>Elementary Operations Preserve Solutions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If one system of linear equations is obtained from another by an elementary
        operation, then the two systems are equivalent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Suppose the new system is obtained from the old one by an elementary operation.
        Every solution of the old system is a solution of the new one, because each
        equation of the new system is built from equations of the old system by
        arithmetic: interchanging equations changes nothing; if numbers satisfy an
        equation then they satisfy any nonzero multiple of it; and if numbers satisfy two
        equations, then they satisfy the sum of one with any multiple of the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the reverse direction, observe that each elementary operation can be undone
        by an elementary operation of the same type: interchange the same two equations
        again, multiply by the reciprocal of the scaling factor, or subtract the same
        multiple that was added. So the old system is obtained from the new one by an
        elementary operation, and by the first paragraph every solution of the new system
        is a solution of the old one. The two systems therefore have exactly the same
        solutions.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    Nothing is lost and nothing is gained <mdash /> which is exactly why we are free to
    apply elementary operations as often as we like, in any order, until the system
    becomes simple enough to read the solutions off.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-elementary-operations">
    <title>The Three Operations in Action</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the system of linear equations
        <md>
          <mrow>
            3x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -x_1 + 3x_2 + 6x_3 \amp = 1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2x_1 \phantom{{}- 2x_2} - x_3 \amp = 3
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Use the three elementary operations, one after another, to eliminate
        <m>x_1</m> from the second equation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The operations are applied <em>in sequence</em>: each one acts on the system
        produced by the one before, not on the original system. This is how they are
        always used in practice.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Interchange the first two equations,</term> <m>E_1 \leftrightarrow E_2</m>,
        to bring the equation with the simplest <m>x_1</m>-term to the top:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            -x_1 + 3x_2 + 6x_3 \amp = 1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            3x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2x_1 \phantom{{}- 2x_2} - x_3 \amp = 3
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Multiply the first equation by <m>-1</m>,</term> <m>-E_1</m>, so that it
        begins with <m>x_1</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x_1 - 3x_2 - 6x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            3x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2x_1 \phantom{{}- 2x_2} - x_3 \amp = 3
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Add <m>-3</m> times the first equation to the second equation,</term>
        <m>-3E_1 + E_2</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x_1 - 3x_2 - 6x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \phantom{x_1 +{}} 7x_2 + 11x_3 \amp = 2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2x_1 \phantom{{}- 2x_2} - x_3 \amp = 3
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Here the second equation became
        <m>(3x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3) - 3(x_1 - 3x_2 - 6x_3) = -1 - 3(-1)</m>, i.e.,
        <m>0x_1 + 7x_2 + 11x_3 = 2</m>. Notice that <m>x_1</m> has been eliminated from
        that equation <mdash /> this is exactly the effect we will exploit systematically.
        By <xref ref="rem-operations-reversible" text="type-global"/>, every system along
        the way is equivalent to the one we started with.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    One thing about this computation is worth noticing. The variable names
    <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m> were carried along at every step but never actually did anything
    <mdash /> all the work was done on the coefficients and constants. The next section
    turns this observation into a bookkeeping device.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-shared-versus-equivalent">
    <title>True or False: Shared Solutions versus Equivalent Systems</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be the first and second systems of
        <xref ref="eg-equivalent-systems" text="type-global" />, and let <m>C</m> and
        <m>D</m> be two more systems:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A: \;\; \begin{array}{rcl} 2x - y \amp = \amp 1 \\ x - y \amp = \amp 0 \end{array}
            \amp \qquad
            B: \;\; \begin{array}{rcl} x + y \amp = \amp 2 \\ y \amp = \amp 1 \end{array}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            C: \;\; \begin{array}{rcl} x + y \amp = \amp 1 \\ 2x + 2y \amp = \amp 2 \end{array}
            \amp \qquad
            D: \;\; \begin{array}{rcl} x - y \amp = \amp 1 \\ 2x - 2y \amp = \amp 2 \end{array}
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Choose <em>True</em> or <em>False</em> for each statement, then select
        <em>Check Me</em> to see feedback. You can change your answer and check again.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="tf-equivalent-chain">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>
          The operations <m>-E_2 + E_1</m>, then <m>-E_1 + E_2</m>, then <m>-E_2</m>, then
          <m>E_2 + E_1</m>, each applied to the system produced by the one before, carry
          system <m>A</m> to system <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          True. Step by step,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \begin{array}{rcl} 2x - y \amp = \amp 1 \\ x - y \amp = \amp 0 \end{array}
              \;\xrightarrow{-E_2 + E_1}\;
              \begin{array}{rcl} x \amp = \amp 1 \\ x - y \amp = \amp 0 \end{array}
              \;\xrightarrow{-E_1 + E_2}\;
              \begin{array}{rcl} x \amp = \amp 1 \\ -y \amp = \amp -1 \end{array}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \;\xrightarrow{-E_2}\;
              \begin{array}{rcl} x \amp = \amp 1 \\ y \amp = \amp 1 \end{array}
              \;\xrightarrow{E_2 + E_1}\;
              \begin{array}{rcl} x + y \amp = \amp 2 \\ y \amp = \amp 1 \end{array}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          The last system is <m>B</m>. By
          <xref ref="rem-operations-reversible" text="type-global" />, each step preserves
          the solution set, so <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are equivalent, and we know this
          without solving either system.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="tf-equivalent-shared-solution">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>
          Systems <m>C</m> and <m>D</m> both have the solution <m>(x, y) = (1, 0)</m>, so
          they are equivalent.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. The pair <m>(0, 1)</m> solves <m>C</m>, since <m>0 + 1 = 1</m> and
          <m>0 + 2 = 2</m>, but not <m>D</m>, since <m>0 - 1 = -1 \neq 1</m>.
          Geometrically, both equations of <m>C</m> describe the line <m>x + y = 1</m> and
          both equations of <m>D</m> describe the line <m>x - y = 1</m>. The two lines
          share only the point <m>(1, 0)</m>, while each system has infinitely many
          solutions. Sharing one solution is not the same as having the same solutions.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="tf-equivalent-unique">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>
          If two systems each have exactly one solution, and it is the same solution for
          both, then the two systems are equivalent.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          True. Each solution set consists of that one solution and nothing else, so the
          two solution sets are equal. This is why the check in
          <xref ref="eg-equivalent-systems" text="type-global" /> was enough: each system
          there was known to have the single solution <m>x = y = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="tf-equivalent-one-inclusion">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>
          To prove that two systems are equivalent, it is enough to show that every
          solution of the first system is also a solution of the second.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. Two systems are equivalent when their solution sets are equal, and that
          requires two inclusions: every solution of the first system solves the second,
          <em>and</em> every solution of the second solves the first. For example, the
          only solution of the system <m>x + y = 1</m>, <m>x - y = 1</m> is <m>(1, 0)</m>,
          which also solves <m>C</m>. But <m>C</m> has other solutions, such as
          <m>(0, 1)</m>, so the two systems are not equivalent.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>
  </example>
</section>
